WEBVTT

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.760
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life from WBEZ Chicago, I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>这是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:33.000
<v Ira Glass>Valentine's Day is around the corner, and here at the show we were talking about romantic comedies and how they don't get a lot of respect. I think it's maybe because of the bad ones. Every part of them just feels too obvious, you know? The couple meets, but they hate each other at first. They go through some things that make them, you know, learn some important lesson about themselves. You know from the very beginning they're going to end up together. And then, no surprise, they do. When it's not done well, it's all too obvious and tired, and you can feel the gears working in the thing.
<v Ira Glass>情人节就要到了，我们节目组一直在谈论浪漫喜剧，以及它们为何得不到应有的尊重。我想这可能是因为烂片太多了。它们每一个部分都显得太刻意了，对吧？男女主角相遇，起初却互相讨厌。他们经历了一些事情，让他们学到了关于自己的人生课题。从一开始你就知道他们最终会在一起。果然不出所料，他们确实在一起了。如果拍得不好，整个剧情就显得过于显眼和乏味，你甚至能感觉到剧本在生硬地运转。

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:47.750
<v Ira Glass>One of the producers on our show, Neil, he wholeheartedly really loves romantic comedies, has favorites that he's watched over and over dozens of times. He once collaborated with the producer of Sleepless in Seattle on a rom-com script that never got made. He has all kinds of thoughts about them.
<v Ira Glass>我们节目的制作人之一尼尔，他由衷地热爱浪漫喜剧，他有几十次反复观看的最爱电影。他曾与《西雅图夜未眠》的制作人合作编写过一部从未投拍的浪漫喜剧剧本。他对这些电影有着各种各样的见解。

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:53.430
<v Ira Glass>And he realized this thing about rom-coms and what's so satisfying about the good ones that I really think is true.
<v Ira Glass>他意识到关于浪漫喜剧的一点，以及优秀浪漫喜剧为何如此令人满足，我认为这确实是事实。

00:00:53.430 --> 00:01:02.070
<v Neil Drumming>I used to say that it was just watching just the close-ups of two beautiful people being funny and clever and witty to each other.
<v Neil Drumming>我以前常说，浪漫喜剧吸引人只是因为看两个漂亮的人在一起搞笑、聪明、风趣。

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:02.810
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:09.960
<v Neil Drumming>Being their sort of best selves, or sometimes worst selves, but then eventually their best selves. And that was kind of enough for me.
<v Neil Drumming>展示他们最好的一面，有时也是最坏的一面，但最终是最好的一面。对我来说，这已经足够了。

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.300
<v Meg Ryan>Now, why shut me out? You know what happens to people who shut everybody out?
<v Meg Ryan>为什么要把我拒之门外？你知道那些把所有人都拒之门外的人会怎样吗？

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.670
<v Kevin Kline>They live the quiet, peaceful lives?
<v Kevin Kline>他们过着安静平和的生活？

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.620
<v Meg Ryan>No, they fester.
<v Meg Ryan>不，他们会溃烂。

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:23.640
<v Ira Glass>That's Meg Ryan and Kevin Kline with a fake French accent in the movie French Kiss.
<v Ira Glass>那是梅格·瑞恩和凯文·克莱恩在电影《情定巴黎》中带着伪造的法国口音。

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.460
<v Kevin Kline>Fester. I am festering.
<v Kevin Kline>溃烂。我正在溃烂。

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:42.720
<v Meg Ryan>Inside, fester and rot. I've seen it happen. You'll become one of those hunched-back lonely old men sitting in the corner of a crowded cafe mumbling to yourself, (FRENCH ACCENT) my ass is twitching. You people make my ass twitch.
<v Meg Ryan>在内心深处，溃烂腐败。我见过这种情况。你会变成那种驼着背、孤独的老人，坐在拥挤咖啡馆的角落里喃喃自语，（法国口音）我的屁股在抽搐。你们这些人让我的屁股抽搐。

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:53.220
<v Neil  Drumming>If there's there's a simple thing that resonates for me, it's that, in the best of these movies, you get to see two people get along in a way that is great.
<v Neil  Drumming>如果有什么简单的事情能引起我的共鸣，那就是在最好的电影里，你能看到两个人以一种很棒的方式相处。

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.120
<v Ira Glass>It's just like nice to see that part of people.
<v Ira Glass>看到人们那温柔的一面真好。

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:10.500
<v Neil  Drumming>Yeah. Yeah. And I think, if you're projecting at all, it's the idea that you can be that connected to someone or receive someone that well. When you see it, you're like, oh, yeah, that would be nice. That's why I like the ones where people spend a lot of time together.
<v Neil  Drumming>是的。是的。我认为，如果你产生了一点共鸣，那是源于这样一种想法：你可以与某人建立那样的连接，或者得到某人那样的回应。当你看到时，你会觉得，哦，对啊，那该多好。这就是为什么我喜欢那些主角花很多时间在一起的电影。

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:39.950
<v Ira Glass>That's key for Neil. In Neil's personal ranking of these films, which has the 2009 film The Ugly Truth at the very, very bottom, the three rom-coms that sit at the very top like the tousled hair above Hugh Grant's head, are French Kiss, Two Weeks Notice, which is a real estate rom-com, and his very favorite-- lots of people's favorite-- When Harry Met Sally. And they're all at the top of the list for this very reason, because of how much time the couple spends together talking. Take When Harry Met Sally.
<v Ira Glass>这对尼尔来说是关键。在尼尔个人的电影排名中，2009年的《丑陋的真相》排在最末，而排在前三名的浪漫喜剧，就像休·格兰特头顶蓬乱的头发一样，分别是《情定巴黎》、《贴身情人》（一部关于房地产的浪漫喜剧），以及他最喜欢的，也是许多人最喜欢的《当哈利遇到莎莉》。它们之所以都排在榜首，正是因为男女主角在一起交谈的时间很多。看看《当哈利遇到莎莉》。

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:54.860
<v Neil Drumming>The thing that I like about it is that at least you get a vision of what their relationship actually is, because they spend 12 years together before they finally get together. So you get to see real fights. You get to see their relationship grow. You get to see their personalities clash.
<v Neil Drumming>我喜欢这部电影的原因是，至少你能看到他们真实的恋爱过程，因为他们在最终走到一起之前，已经在一起相处了12年。所以你能看到真实的争吵。你能看到他们关系的成长。你能看到他们个性的碰撞。

00:02:54.860 --> 00:03:13.210
<v Neil Drumming>And so they actually have a chance to fall in love by talking to each other, as opposed to, in romantic comedies now, or like where there's the montage and music playing and then you're just supposed to come out of that thinking they're in love, they actually spend time with each other. Like there's a sequence of scenes in When Harry Met Sally where they're just getting along, and they're just talking on the phone.
<v Neil Drumming>他们确实有机会通过交谈相爱，而不是像现在的浪漫喜剧那样，只有一段配着音乐的蒙太奇镜头，你就默认他们相爱了。他们是真正花时间陪伴彼此。比如《当哈利遇到莎莉》里有一系列场景，他们只是相处得很好，在电话里聊天。

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.480
<v Meg  Ryan>Hello?
<v Meg  Ryan>喂？

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.050
<v Billy  Crystal>You sleeping?
<v Billy  Crystal>你睡了吗？

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.540
<v Meg  Ryan>No, I was watching Casablanca.
<v Meg  Ryan>没，我在看《卡萨布兰卡》。

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:22.590
<v Billy Crystal>Channel, please.
<v Billy Crystal>频道，谢谢。

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:23.740
<v Meg Ryan>11.
<v Meg Ryan>11频道。

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:29.360
<v Billy Crystal>Thank you. Got it. Now, you're telling me you would be happier with Victor Laszlow than with Humphrey Bogart?
<v Billy Crystal>谢谢。收到了。现在，你告诉我你会觉得和维克多·拉兹洛在一起比和亨弗莱·鲍嘉在一起更幸福？

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:30.930
<v Meg Ryan>When did I say that?
<v Meg Ryan>我什么时候说过那话？

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.200
<v Billy Crystal>When we drove to New York.
<v Billy Crystal>我们开车去纽约的时候。

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.350
<v Meg Ryan>I never said that. I would never have said that.
<v Meg Ryan>我从来没说过。我绝不可能那样说。

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:39.210
<v Billy Crystal>All right, fine. Have it your way. Have you been sleeping?
<v Billy Crystal>好吧，随你便。你一直在睡觉吗？

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:40.360
<v Meg Ryan>Why?
<v Meg Ryan>为什么？

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:41.970
<v Billy Crystal>Because I haven't been sleeping.
<v Billy Crystal>因为我一直睡不着。

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:58.000
<v Neil  Drumming>I think if I was dating someone who hated When Harry Met Sally, I don't know that I could date them. Like, [LAUGHS] I don't know that I could. If you're not interested in the relationship between Harry and Sally, I don't really understand what kind of person you are. [LAUGHS] I don't know.
<v Neil  Drumming>我觉得如果我和一个讨厌《当哈利遇到莎莉》的人约会，我不知道我是否能和他们继续下去。（笑）我不知道能不能。如果你对哈利和莎莉的关系不感兴趣，我真的不知道你是哪种人。（笑）我不知道。

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:58.670
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:06.450
<v Neil Drumming>That's not to say that you're a bad person, it just means I don't think I understand you.
<v Neil Drumming>这并不是说你是个坏人，只是意味着我觉得我无法理解你。

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:35.540
<v Ira   Glass>OK. Romantic comedies are contrived. The people are way more clever and way better looking than in real life. The stories are full of things that would be ridiculous and sometimes maybe even on the stalker-y side if they happened to any of us. But this totally artificial form, when it works, reminds you of what it feels like to be in love and of somebody who wants to listen to what you say and who says things that you want to listen to. And so for this Valentine's Day, we're devoting our show to rom-coms.
<v Ira   Glass>好吧。浪漫喜剧确实很刻意。电影里的人比现实生活中更聪明、更好看。故事里充满了如果发生在我们身上会显得荒谬，甚至有些跟踪狂倾向的情节。但这种完全虚构的形式，在运作良好时，会让你想起恋爱是什么感觉，想起有一个人愿意倾听你说话，并且会说出你想听的话是什么感觉。所以在这个情人节，我们把节目献给浪漫喜剧。

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:52.150
<v Ira   Glass>In each of our acts today, we found a story that reminds us of some aspect of a movie rom-com. And yes, we did go on a search for stories of people running, sprinting down the street in real life in an urgent rush to tell someone that they love them. Stay with us.
<v Ira   Glass>在我们今天的每一个章节里，我们都找到了一个让我们想起浪漫喜剧电影某个侧面的故事。没错，我们确实去寻找了在现实生活中，人们为了告诉心爱的人“我爱你”而急切奔跑、冲刺在大街上的故事。请继续收听。

00:04:52.150 --> 00:05:05.100
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Meet Cute. So the first thing a rom-com needs is for the couple to meet in an appealing way, the meet cute.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，初次相遇。浪漫喜剧的第一要素就是男女主角要以一种引人入胜的方式相遇，即“初遇”。

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:09.970
<v Neil Drumming>The meet cute is supposed to make you feel like no matter what happens, these two people should be together.
<v Neil Drumming>初遇应该让你感觉到，无论发生什么，这两个人都注定要在一起。

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:35.030
<v Ira Glass>There are so many ways to do this. In The Wedding Planner, Matthew McConaughey saves Jennifer Lopez from a runaway dumpster that is rolling down the street. In Pretty Woman, Richard Gere gets lost in a very fancy car and Julia Roberts gives him directions. Bringing Up Baby, Katherine Hepburn picks up Cary Grant's golf ball on a golf course. In Reality Bites, Winona Ryder throws her cigarette into Ben Stiller's car. And Neil's favorite, When Harry Met Sally--
<v Ira Glass>达成这种效果的方式有很多。《爱上新郎》中，马修·麦康纳从滚下街头的垃圾箱里救了詹妮弗·洛佩兹。《风月俏佳人》中，理查·基尔开着豪车迷了路，朱莉娅·罗伯茨为他指路。《育婴奇谭》中，凯瑟琳·赫本在球场捡起了加里·格兰特的球。《四个毕业生》中，薇诺娜·瑞德把烟头扔进了本·斯蒂勒的车里。而尼尔最爱的《当哈利遇到莎莉》——

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:55.560
<v Neil Drumming>The thing about When Harry Met Sally is it has an extended meet cute, which I like. They take a road trip from Chicago to New York, and so they're kind of meeting for several hours. And from the very beginning, it's contentious because Harry has all these theories about relationships that she finds like crazy and off-putting.
<v Neil Drumming>关于《当哈利遇到莎莉》，我喜欢的是它有一个延长的初遇过程。他们从芝加哥开车到纽约，所以他们实际上相处了好几个小时。从一开始就充满火药味，因为哈利对人际关系有一套理论，而莎莉觉得那很疯狂且令人反感。

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:04.710
<v Billy Crystal>What I'm saying is-- and this is not a come on in any way, shape, or form-- is that men and women can't be friends because the sex part always gets in the way.
<v Billy Crystal>我要说的是——这绝不是为了搭讪——男人和女人无法成为朋友，因为性总是会横插一脚。

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:10.500
<v Meg       Ryan>That's not true. I have a number of men friends and there is no sex involved.
<v Meg       Ryan>那不是真的。我有很多男性朋友，我们之间根本没有涉及性。

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:11.370
<v Billy Crystal>No, you don't.
<v Billy Crystal>不，你没有。

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:12.090
<v Meg Ryan>Yes, I do.
<v Meg Ryan>我有。

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:12.890
<v Billy Crystal>No, you don't.
<v Billy Crystal>不，你没有。

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:13.730
<v Meg Ryan>Yes, I do.
<v Meg Ryan>我有。

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:15.460
<v Billy Crystal>You only think you do.
<v Billy Crystal>你只是以为你有。

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.110
<v Meg Ryan>You're saying I'm having sex with these men without my knowledge?
<v Meg Ryan>你的意思是，我是在自己不知情的情况下和这些男人发生了性关系吗？

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:20.640
<v Billy Crystal>No, what I'm saying is they all want to have sex with you.
<v Billy Crystal>不，我的意思是他们都想和你发生性关系。

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:21.830
<v Meg Ryan>They do not.
<v Meg Ryan>他们不想。

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:22.600
<v Billy Crystal>Do too.
<v Billy Crystal>他们想。

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:23.570
<v Meg Ryan>They do not.
<v Meg Ryan>他们不想。

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:24.700
<v Billy Crystal>Do too.
<v Billy Crystal>他们想。

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:32.220
<v Neil Drumming>I think it sets up the chemistry. Like it sets up that there is something about them that you want to root for.
<v Neil Drumming>我认为这建立了化学反应。这确立了他们身上有某种让你想要支持他们的特质。

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:46.090
<v Ira Glass>Meet cutes do happen in real life, but for this first story in today's show, we have a piece of fiction, a story where the meet cute plays an important role in the story as a turning point for one of the characters. The story's by Simon Rich. The actor Daniel Radcliffe read it for us.
<v Ira Glass>初遇在现实中确实会发生，但今天节目的第一个故事是一篇小说，一个初遇在故事中作为角色转折点的故事。故事作者是西蒙·里奇。演员丹尼尔·雷德克里夫为我们朗读。

00:06:46.090 --> 00:07:05.960
<v Daniel Radcliffe>"I don't understand," Professor Xander Kaplan said while his girlfriend sobbed into a pillow. "I thought you liked tulips." "I do," she said. "It's just-- you get them for me every year. It's starting to get a little impersonal. I mean, this time, you didn't even include a card."
<v Daniel Radcliffe>“我不明白，”赞德·卡普兰教授看着女友埋在枕头里啜泣，“我以为你喜欢郁金香。”“我是喜欢，”她说，“只是……你每年都送这个，开始觉得有点没诚意了。我的意思是，这次你甚至连卡片都没放。”

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:33.310
<v Daniel Radcliffe>Xander winced. Her reasoning was sound. "I apologize," he said. "I obviously made an error in judgment." He tried to take her hand, but she pulled it out of reach. "Do you remember what I did for your birthday?" She said, "I got you that new Bunsen burner you wanted. I knit you a pair of wool socks so your feet wouldn't get cold in the lab. You never make that kind of effort for me. All you do is think about yourself."
<v Daniel Radcliffe>赞德畏缩了。她的理由很有道理。“我道歉，”他说，“我显然判断失误了。”他试图去拉她的手，但她躲开了。“你记得你为我的生日做了什么吗？”她说，“我给你买了你想要的那个本生灯。我为你织了一双羊毛袜，这样你在实验室里脚就不会冷。你从来不会为我付出那样的努力。你只关心你自己。”

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:58.970
<v Daniel Radcliffe>"That's incorrect," Xander said. "What about Emiladium? It took me nine months to synthesize that element and I named it after you." "You were going to synthesize that element anyway," Emily said. "You needed it for your secret project, that silver orb thing in your lab. Emiladium wasn't about me, it was about you. I mean, for god sakes, you won't even tell me what it does."
<v Daniel Radcliffe>“这不准确，”赞德说，“埃米利亚元素呢？我花了九个月才合成出那个元素，而且是以你的名字命名的。”“你反正都要合成那个元素，”艾米丽说，“你需要它来完成你的秘密计划，就是你实验室里那个银色的球状物。埃米利亚元素不是关于我的，是关于你自己的。老天，你甚至不告诉我它是干什么用的。”

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:18.500
<v Daniel Radcliffe>Xander sighed. "Is there anything I can do to make it up to you?" Emily blinked back some tears. "I don't know. I mean, it's not like you can just go back in time and get me a different present." Xander's expression brightened. "Wait there," he said. "I'll be right back."
<v Daniel Radcliffe>赞德叹了口气。“我能做些什么来补偿你吗？”艾米丽眨去泪水。“我不知道。我的意思是，你又不能回到过去给我换个礼物。”赞德的表情亮了起来。“在那儿等着，”他说，“我马上回来。”

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:41.370
<v Daniel Radcliffe>Xander hurried down the hall, crept into his laboratory, and locked the door behind him. His time machine was right where he had left it. He climbed inside the silver orb and flicked on the power switch. His plan was simple, travel back in time to this morning, find a new gift for Emily, and bring it to the present. But there were a couple of risks.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>赞德匆忙穿过走廊，溜进实验室，反锁了门。他的时间机器就在他离开的地方。他爬进银色球体，拨动了电源开关。他的计划很简单：回到今天早上，为艾米丽找一份新礼物，然后带到现代。但有几个风险。

00:08:41.370 --> 00:09:01.480
<v Daniel Radcliffe>There was a chance, for example, that using the machine would cause the universe to explode. He'd never tested the thing out before. There was also no guarantee that he would be able to find a good present. He only had enough Emiladium to fuel five minutes of time travel. That didn't give him a lot of wiggle room. Wherever he went, he would have to shop efficiently.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>例如，使用机器可能会导致宇宙爆炸。他以前从未测试过这玩意儿。此外，他也不能保证能找到一份好礼物。他剩下的埃米利亚元素只够进行五分钟的时间旅行。这并没有给他留下多少周旋余地。无论他去哪儿，都必须高效地购物。

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:25.570
<v Daniel Radcliffe>Xander was usually a pretty good problem solver. He had, for example, invented a time machine. But quantum physics and nuclear hydraulics were trivial compared to the rigors of gift shopping. He massaged his temples, trying to remember if Emily had dropped any hints lately. He vaguely recalled her staring at a vase in Crate & Barrel, but that place was full of vases. There was no way he'd be able to pick out the right one.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>赞德通常是一个相当不错的解题者。比如，他发明了时间机器。但量子物理和核水力学与选礼物的严格要求相比，简直微不足道。他揉了揉太阳穴，试图回忆艾米丽最近是否透露过什么线索。他模糊地想起她盯着Crate & Barrel商店里的一只花瓶看，但那地方到处都是花瓶。他不可能选出正确的那一个。

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:41.070
<v Daniel Radcliffe>He was trying to remember the name of her favorite perfume when a thought entered his head. Maybe he was thinking too small. His machine could transport him to any time and place in human history. Why go back a few hours when he could go back a few centuries?
<v Daniel Radcliffe>他正试图想起她最喜欢的香水名字时，一个念头进入了他的脑海。也许他想得太狭隘了。他的机器可以将他传送到人类历史上的任何时间和地点。为什么只回去几个小时，而不是回去几个世纪呢？

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:58.830
<v Daniel Radcliffe>He knew Emily loved Shakespeare. She'd written her senior thesis on one of his tragedies. Why not travel back to the Globe Theater and swipe her an original script? It wouldn't be too difficult, he reasoned. All he'd have to do is dash back backstage and grab one. It would be the most impressive gift she'd ever received in her life.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>他知道艾米丽喜欢莎士比亚。她的大四论文就是关于他的一部悲剧。为什么不回到环球剧场去顺走她的原始剧本呢？他推断，这不会太难。他只需要冲进后台抢走一本。这将是她一生中收到的最令人印象深刻的礼物。

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:12.520
<v Daniel Radcliffe>But which tragedy had Emily written her thesis about? He knew it was one of the king ones, Richard the something or Charles the something, but there are a bunch of those. What if he got it wrong? It was too risky.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>但艾米丽的论文写的是哪部悲剧？他知道是那些国王系列中的一部，理查什么或者查理什么，但那有一大堆。如果拿错了怎么办？这太冒险了。

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:27.520
<v Daniel Radcliffe>There was always jewelry. He knew the general construction dates for King Tut's tomb. He could park in front of the pyramid, run inside, and snatch a jade stone. He entered the coordinates and was about to push the lever when he started to second guess himself again.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>还有珠宝。他知道图坦卡蒙墓的大致建造日期。他可以停在金字塔前，跑进去抢一块翡翠。他输入了坐标，正要按下操纵杆时，他又开始动摇了。

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:38.430
<v Daniel Radcliffe>Buying women jewelry was always chancy. Emily had very specific tastes, and what if she didn't like jade? It wasn't like he'd be able to go back and return it.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>给女人买珠宝总是很冒险。艾米丽有非常独特的品味，如果她不喜欢翡翠怎么办？他又不能回去退货。

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:55.460
<v Daniel Radcliffe>He thought back to the night they met. He was finishing his PhD at the time, and his lab had closed early because of Easter. He'd stuffed his papers into his briefcase and shuffled through the rain to the 116th Street Station. It was 4:05 AM and the platform was deserted, except for Emily.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>他回想起他们相遇的那天晚上。那时他正在攻读博士学位，他的实验室因为复活节提前关闭了。他把文件塞进公文包，冒雨挪到了116街车站。当时是凌晨4:05，除了艾米丽，站台上空无一人。

00:10:55.460 --> 00:11:21.300
<v Daniel Radcliffe>It had been several days since Xander's last conversation with a human, and when she started to speak to him, he felt the stirrings of a panic attack. But Emily's friendly smile managed, somehow, to put him at ease. She was awfully cheerful, given her circumstances. Her MetroCard had expired, she said, and the machines were broken. She'd been stranded for over 20 minutes. Would he be willing to sell her a ride?
<v Daniel Radcliffe>距离赞德上一次与人交谈已经过去好几天了，当她开始和他说话时，他感到了一阵恐慌。但艾米丽友好的微笑不知怎的让他放松了下来。考虑到当时的情况，她出奇地乐观。她说她的地铁卡过期了，而且售票机坏了。她已经被困了20多分钟。他愿意帮她刷一下卡吗？

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:31.350
<v Daniel Radcliffe>Xander nodded and watched as she rooted around in her purse for some cash to pay him back. It was a moment or two before it occurred to him that she had given him the chance to be gallant.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>赞德点点头，看着她在钱包里翻找现金想还他钱。过了一会儿，他才意识到，她给了他一个展示骑士风度的机会。

00:11:31.350 --> 00:12:05.680
<v Daniel Radcliffe>"You-- you don't have to reimburse me," he said. "I'll swipe you in for free." She thanked him enthusiastically and then, shockingly, wrapped her arms around his torso. Xander wasn't used to physical contact. And although the hug was brief, it caused his entire body to tingle from head to toe. It was a startling sensation, like walking through an electrically-charged field. He still felt that way whenever she touched him.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>“你……你不需要还我钱，”他说，“我免费帮你刷。”她热情地感谢了他，然后，令人震惊地，她抱住了他的上半身。赞德不习惯身体接触。虽然拥抱很短暂，但却让他全身从头到脚感到刺痛。这是一种令人吃惊的感觉，就像走过一个带电的磁场。每当她触摸他时，他依然有这种感觉。

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:27.540
<v Daniel Radcliffe>Xander was an atheist and believed fiercely in random causality. But by the end of their shared subway ride, he was sure he'd experienced a miracle. This wonderful person had shown up out of nowhere and given him a chance at love. And in return, he'd given her three years of misery.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>赞德是一名无神论者，坚信随机因果关系。但在共享地铁旅程结束时，他确定自己经历了一个奇迹。这个美好的人凭空出现，给了他一次恋爱的机会。而作为回报，他却给了她三年的痛苦。

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:43.500
<v Daniel Radcliffe>He thought about all of his Saturday nights at the lab, ignoring her calls, making excuses. He thought about the way she cried when he handed her the tulips. How could he make up for three years of romantic ineptitude with a single birthday present?
<v Daniel Radcliffe>他想起自己所有在实验室度过的周六晚上，无视她的电话，编造借口。他想起当他把郁金香递给她时，她哭泣的样子。他怎么能用一个生日礼物来弥补三年的浪漫无能呢？

00:12:43.500 --> 00:13:16.570
<v Daniel Radcliffe>He closed his eyes and concentrated. There had to be a right answer. Cleopatra's crown. Joan of Arc's sword. A baby dinosaur. What was the greatest thing he could give her, the very best present in the world? It was the hardest problem he'd ever attempted to solve. But then, as always, the solution came to him.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>他闭上眼睛，集中精神。一定有一个正确的答案。克利奥帕特拉的皇冠。贞德的剑。一只恐龙宝宝。什么才是他能给她的最伟大的东西，世界上最好的礼物？这是他尝试解决过的最难的问题。但随后，一如既往，解决方案浮现在他脑海中。

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:34.650
<v Daniel Radcliffe>Xander parked his time machine by the 116th Street Station and dashed into the subway. It was 3:45 AM a little over three years in the past. Emily was standing by the turnstile, swiping and re-swiping her expired MetroCard. He took a deep breath and approached her.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>赞德把他的时间机器停在116街车站，冲进地铁。现在是凌晨3:45，也就是三年多以前。艾米丽正站在旋转闸门旁，一遍又一遍地刷她那张过期的地铁卡。他深吸一口气，走近她。

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:55.650
<v Daniel Radcliffe>"Let me guess," he said. "Expired MetroCard?" She chuckled. "How'd you know?" "I had a hunch. Come on, I'll swipe you through." "Oh, that's OK," she said. "I'll just go to the machine upstairs or--" "The machines are all broken," he said. "You'd better catch this one," he said. "The next one won't come for another 20 minutes."
<v Daniel Radcliffe>“让我猜猜，”他说，“地铁卡过期了？”她笑了笑。“你怎么知道？”“我有种预感。来吧，我帮你刷过去。”“哦，没关系，”她说，“我上去找售票机或者……”“售票机都坏了，”他说，“你最好赶上这一班，”他说，“下一班要等20分钟。”

00:13:55.650 --> 00:14:12.050
<v Daniel Radcliffe>Before she could protest, he took out his MetroCard and swiped her through the turnstile. She smiled back at him with confusion. "Aren't you coming?" she asked as the train pulled into the station. Xander averted his eyes. He worried that if he looked at her, he would start to cry.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>在她抗议之前，他拿出地铁卡，帮她刷过了闸门。她困惑地回以微笑。“你不一起吗？”当列车驶入车站时她问道。赞德避开了目光。他担心如果看着她，他会哭出来。

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:30.210
<v Daniel Radcliffe>"I need to take a different train," he said. "Well, at least let me pay you for the--" "That's all right," he said, his voice breaking. "It's a present."
<v Daniel Radcliffe>“我得换乘另一趟车，”他说。“好吧，那至少让我付你……”“没关系，”他说，声音哽咽，“这是一个礼物。”

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:56.840
<v Daniel Radcliffe>He was about to turn away when she leaned over the turnstile and hugged him. It was exactly as he remembered it, her long brown hair brushing softly against his neck, his entire body tingling with warmth. "Thanks," she said. He tried to say, you're welcome, but the words got caught in his throat. He waved goodbye as she boarded the train, then he marched out of the station alone.
<v Daniel Radcliffe>他正要转身离开时，她俯身越过闸门拥抱了他。这和他记忆中一模一样，她长长的棕色头发轻柔地掠过他的脖子，他全身充满了温暖。谢谢，她说。他想说，不客气，但话语卡在了喉咙里。她登上列车时，他挥手告别，然后独自一人走出了车站。

00:14:56.840 --> 00:15:30.850
<v Ira    Glass>Daniel Radcliffe reading the short story, "The Present" by Simon Rich from Rich's book, The Last Girlfriend on Earth. Radcliffe is also the star of Simon Rich's new TV show, Miracle Workers, which will be out soon on TBS. And he stars in a new film, Beast of Burden that comes out this month.
<v Ira    Glass>丹尼尔·雷德克里夫朗读了西蒙·里奇的短篇小说《礼物》，摘自里奇的书《地球上最后一个女友》。雷德克里夫也是西蒙·里奇新电视剧《奇迹缔造者》的主演，该剧即将于TBS播出。他还是本月上映的新电影《困兽》的主演。

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:36.180
<v Ira  Glass>Act Two, The Obstacle.
<v Ira  Glass>第二幕，障碍。

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:48.360
<v Neil Drumming>The main body of most romantic comedies is there's something or a group of things keeping them apart. There's always obstacles that are keeping these two people who are fated to be together apart.
<v Neil Drumming>大多数浪漫喜剧的主体都是某种或一群障碍阻碍了男女主角。总是有障碍让这两个注定要在一起的人分离。

00:15:48.360 --> 00:16:05.500
<v Ira Glass>The obstacles can be big or little. Tom Hanks isn't over his dead ex-wife in Sleepless in Seattle. In Notting Hill, a guy falls in love with somebody who's too famous for him. In Bridget Jones, she's going after the wrong guy, which, of course, is Pride and Prejudice, and, I don't know, so many films. It's one of the most common.
<v Ira Glass>障碍可大可小。《西雅图夜未眠》中，汤姆·汉克斯走不出亡妻的阴影。《诺丁山》中，一个普通人爱上了一个对他来说太出名的人。《BJ单身日记》中，她追错了对象，当然，这源自《傲慢与偏见》，天知道还有多少电影。这是最常见的情节之一。

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:28.310
<v Ira Glass>In When Harry Met Sally, they each are involved with other people for a lot of the film. But the real obstacle is that they're friends, which, in this film, has a special meaning, because if you remember when Harry met Sally on that car ride originally, he told her that he didn't think that men and women could ever just be friends. So this is new for him. Billy Crystal has a scene in the film with a sidekick character, a best friend, played by Bruno Kirby, where they talk about this.
<v Ira Glass>在《当哈利遇到莎莉》中，他们在电影的大部分时间里都和别人有牵扯。但真正的障碍是他们是朋友，这在电影中有着特殊的意义，因为如果你记得当初哈利在车里见到莎莉时，他告诉她他认为男人和女人不可能仅仅是朋友。所以这对他是全新的。比利·克里斯托在电影中有一个与布鲁诺·柯比饰演的挚友互动的场景，他们谈到了这一点。

00:16:28.310 --> 00:16:30.320
<v Bruno Kirby>I don't understand this relationship.
<v Bruno Kirby>我不理解这种关系。

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:31.240
<v Billy Crystal>What do you mean?
<v Billy Crystal>你指什么？

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:32.780
<v Bruno Kirby>You enjoy being with her?
<v Bruno Kirby>你喜欢和她在一起？

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:33.470
<v Billy Crystal>Yeah.
<v Billy Crystal>是啊。

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:34.590
<v Bruno Kirby>You find her attractive?
<v Bruno Kirby>你觉得她有吸引力？

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:35.310
<v Billy Crystal>Yeah.
<v Billy Crystal>是啊。

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:36.670
<v Bruno Kirby>And you're not sleeping with her?
<v Bruno Kirby>那你没跟她上床？

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:37.300
<v Billy Crystal>No.
<v Billy Crystal>没有。

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:39.660
<v Bruno Kirby>You're afraid to let yourself be happy.
<v Bruno Kirby>你害怕让自己幸福。

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:47.540
<v Billy Crystal>Why can't you give me credit for this? This is a big thing for me. I never had a relationship with a woman that didn't involve sex. I feel like I'm growing.
<v Billy Crystal>为什么你不能肯定我这一点？这对我很重要。我以前和女人的关系从来没离过性。我觉得我在成长。

00:16:47.540 --> 00:17:05.770
<v Ira Glass>We went out looking for a real-life couple facing some obstacle that kept them from being together, and that's not actually very hard to find. But one of our producers, Elna Baker, heard about a couple where the obstacle that confronted them, once the relationship got going, was pretty unusual. It was a couple one of her friends was in years ago.
<v Ira Glass>我们去找了一对现实中面临障碍的恋人，这其实并不难找。但我们的制作人之一，埃尔娜·贝克，听说了一对情侣，一旦关系开始，他们面临的障碍非常不同寻常。那是她几年前的一位朋友的经历。

00:17:05.770 --> 00:17:18.740
<v Ira Glass>Quick warning to everybody who's listening to this podcast version of our show, there are some words that we have un-beeped in this and other stories in the program. If you don't want to hear that, maybe you're listening with kids, you can get a beeped version at our website, thisamericanlife.org. Anyway, here's Elna.
<v Ira Glass>向收听此播客版本节目的各位提个醒，在这个故事和其他节目中，我们保留了一些未经消音的词汇。如果你不想听到这些，或者和孩子一起收听，可以在我们的网站 thisamericanlife.org 获取消音版本。总之，这是埃尔娜。

00:17:18.740 --> 00:17:46.520
<v Elna  Baker>My friend, Michelle Buteau, is one of the most audacious, ballsy people I know. And she brings this attitude into all aspects of her life, including relationships. This is a story about her and her boyfriend. It starts in the '90s, when she was 18, going to college, living in Miami. And as college students do, she adopted a new cool persona for herself, one that wore dark lipstick, cargo pants, and danced in reggae clubs every weekend.
<v Elna  Baker>我的朋友米歇尔·布托是我认识的最胆大、最强悍的人之一。她把这种态度带到了生活的方方面面，包括恋爱。这是一个关于她和男友的故事。故事始于90年代，当时她18岁，在上大学，住在迈阿密。正如大学生那样，她为自己采用了一个新的酷人设，穿着深色口红、工装裤，每个周末都在雷鬼俱乐部跳舞。

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:56.300
<v Michelle Buteau>I loved dancing. I wanted to be a fly girl on In Living Color. I definitely would have been Snoop Dogg's video ho if I had the chance.
<v Michelle Buteau>我热爱跳舞。我想成为《In Living Color》里的时尚女孩。如果我有机会，我绝对会当Snoop Dogg的MV舞女。

00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:57.480
<v Elna Baker>Yeah.
<v Elna Baker>是啊。

00:17:57.480 --> 00:18:21.580
<v Michelle Buteau>And, yeah, I was out one night at a teeny bopper club, and that's where I saw him. I remember the smoke machine was working. It felt like we were at a bar mitzvah somewhere in Jersey. And literally, when the smoke cleared, I'm like, who is that tall boy with the khakis on and the big gold chain and the curly hair? How do I talk to him?
<v Michelle Buteau>有一天晚上，我在一个青少年舞厅，就在那儿看到了他。我记得烟雾机开着。感觉就像是在泽西岛的某个犹太受戒仪式上。烟雾散去后，我心想，那个穿着卡其裤、戴着大金链、留着卷发的长腿男孩是谁？我该怎么跟他搭讪？

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:44.220
<v Michelle Buteau>And I kind of just sort of like inched my way over to him on the dance floor. I remember doing this move where we're like-- we both sort of like roll into each other's body, and I can get a whiff of his Drakkar Noir and I was like, mm, yes, yes, yes. And my heart was beating so fast, I could hear it. And I'm like, oh, my god, this is what love is. I feel like a Puerto Rican Molly Ringwald.
<v Michelle Buteau>我就在舞池里一点点蹭过去。我记得做了一个动作，我们就像……我们两个人的身体滚动到了一起，我闻到了他身上德拉卡·诺尔香水的味道，我心里想，嗯，就是这样。我的心脏跳得飞快，我都能听到心跳声。我心想，天哪，这就是爱情。我觉得自己像个波多黎各版的莫莉·林沃德。

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:51.830
<v Elna         Baker>Like in any good first encounter, there were magical coincidences.
<v Elna         Baker>像所有美好的初遇一样，有一些神奇的巧合。

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:59.080
<v Michelle Buteau>We were walking out to the parking lot, and we realized we both had the same car, Mazda Protegees, both leased by our moms.
<v Michelle Buteau>我们走到停车场时，意识到我们开了同一款车，马自达Protegee，都是我们妈妈租的。

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:02.880
<v Elna Baker>Was that a moment where you're like, this is fate? I mean, not only did I go to this club--
<v Elna Baker>那一刻你觉得这是缘分吗？我的意思是，不仅仅是我去了那个俱乐部——

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:11.530
<v Michelle Buteau>Oh, definitely. I'm like, what? Like, out of all the cars in the world, out of all the dance clubs, you and me.
<v Michelle Buteau>哦，肯定的。我心想，什么鬼？在这世界上所有的车里，在所有的舞厅里，竟然是你和我。

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:15.070
<v Elna Baker>He was 18, too, but he seemed really grown up.
<v Elna Baker>他也是18岁，但他看起来真的很成熟。

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:28.630
<v Michelle Buteau>He worked at Best Buy selling DVDs, and he was also a drug dealer. And in my mind, I was like, oh, my god, he's so cool. He's so good at math. He counts so quickly.
<v Michelle Buteau>他在百思买卖DVD，同时也是个毒贩。我当时心里想，天哪，他太酷了。他数学真好。他数钱数得真快。

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:28.880
<v Michelle Buteau>[LAUGHTER]
<v Michelle Buteau>（笑声）

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:59.240
<v Elna Baker>In case you're thinking, drug dealer, red flag, he was barely a drug dealer. He dealt weed. Nearly every woman I know has dated a weed dealer. They quickly got serious. And he was Michelle's first big relationship. He was funny, smart. They had good banter. And he was this incredibly accepting person, made her feel comfortable, confident about her body and about sex, which she'd never really felt before. She imagined a real future with him.
<v Elna Baker>如果你还在想，毒贩？红旗警报。他几乎不算毒贩。他只卖大麻。我认识的几乎每个女人都和卖大麻的人约会过。他们的关系很快变得认真起来。他是米歇尔的第一段重要恋情。他有趣、聪明。他们很聊得来。他是一个极其包容的人，让她感到舒适，对自己的身体和性生活充满自信，这是她以前从未感受过的。她想象着和他拥有真实的未来。

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:28.710
<v Michelle  Buteau>The plan was-- I mean, looking back on it, it seems so basic, but just to be with each other, and to have fun, and to have kids, and to go out to dinner after a movie on a Friday night, you know, the Olive Garden, Cheesecake Factory, just kind of exist in a really cute apartment with beige carpet and white blinds. Yeah, the American Dream.
<v Michelle  Buteau>计划是——回想起来，这似乎太基本了，但就是在一起，享受乐趣，生孩子，周五晚上电影后出去吃晚餐，你知道，Olive Garden，芝士蛋糕工厂，就那样住在公寓里，有米色地毯和白色百叶窗。是的，这就是美国梦。

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:44.230
<v Elna  Baker>Fast forward three years into the relationship. Their lives are totally entwined. Their families are close. They vacation together. It was that point of no return place in a relationship where you're just like, here it is. This is it.
