00:20:44.230 --> 00:21:01.750
<v Elna  Baker>But there was this one thing that seemed sort of off, a lack of photographic evidence, no pictures of himself as a kid, specifically no school pictures, no prom picture. Even his mom didn't have any shots of him. It was just weird. And then one night--
<v Elna  Baker>但有一件事显得有点不对劲，缺乏照片证据，没有他小时候的照片，特别是没有学校照片、没有毕业舞会照片。甚至他妈妈都没有他的照片。这真的很奇怪。然后有一天晚上——

00:21:01.750 --> 00:21:22.060
<v Michelle Buteau>I had a dream that he called me up. He told me that he never graduated high school. When I called him the next day and I was like I had this crazy dream, he just started crying. I could just hear him gently sobbing and he's like, not only did I not graduate high school, I don't even know how to read.
<v Michelle Buteau>我做梦他给我打电话。他在梦里告诉我他高中从没毕业。第二天我给他打电话说我做了一个疯狂的梦时，他开始哭。我能听到他轻轻啜泣，他说，我不仅没高中毕业，我甚至根本不识字。

00:21:22.060 --> 00:21:28.990
<v Michelle Buteau>I'm just like, how? Like, I thought I knew you. Like, what? Like, haven't we read something together?
<v Michelle Buteau>我当时就想，怎么可能？我觉得我了解你。这太不可思议了。我们难道没一起读过什么东西吗？

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:37.390
<v Elna Baker>Their entire relationship flashed before her eyes, one moment after another. Suddenly, it all made sense.
<v Elna Baker>他们恋爱的一幕幕瞬间在她眼前闪过。突然，一切都说得通了。

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:49.600
<v Michelle Buteau>And I was going back. You know, I realized, like, oh my god, this is why we go to the same restaurant. We would go to the same restaurant, and he'd order the same thing because he couldn't read the menu.
<v Michelle Buteau>我开始回想。我意识到，天哪，这就是为什么我们总是去同一家餐厅。我们会去同一家餐厅，他点一样的菜，因为他根本看不懂菜单。

00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:50.570
<v Elna Baker>Oh, wow.
<v Elna Baker>哦，哇。

00:21:50.570 --> 00:22:05.150
<v Michelle Buteau>And he liked you go to restaurants that had pictures of the food. You know, I would write him poems and stuff and he's like, read them to me. You know, it's better when I hear it from you. And never wanted to go through his mail. I had to help him.
<v Michelle Buteau>他喜欢去有食物照片的餐馆。你知道，我给他写诗什么的，他会说，读给我听。你知道，听你读更好。而且他从不想处理邮件。我不得不帮他。

00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:18.790
<v Elna  Baker>When you stop and think about it, the fact that he'd been able to navigate the world convincingly and keep this from Michelle for over three years, it was an incredible feat. He must have been covering this up constantly.
<v Elna  Baker>当你静下来思考时，他能令人信服地应付世界并瞒着米歇尔三年多，这是一项了不起的成就。他一定一直在时刻掩盖这一点。

00:22:18.790 --> 00:22:48.000
<v Michelle  Buteau>When he told me why he didn't know how to read, it just made my heart break even more. I mean, his dad died when he was young, and so his mom had to work three jobs. And he was depressed and just dropped out of the fifth grade, and nobody ever noticed. His mom didn't want to deal with it. And you know, I'm not judging because it must been so hard for her.
<v Michelle  Buteau>当他告诉我为什么他不识字时，我的心更碎了。他父亲在他很小的时候就去世了，所以他妈妈不得不做三份工作。他很抑郁，五年级就辍学了，没人注意到。他妈妈不想管这事儿。而且，我不评判，因为这对她来说一定很难。

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:57.970
<v Michelle  Buteau>The only thing that kept going through my mind, I remember, was, I want to save you. I want to help you. I want to make this better. You know, we're going to get back on track to what we had planned.
<v Michelle  Buteau>我记得我脑子里唯一闪过的念头是，我想拯救你。我想帮助你。我想让情况好转。我们要回到我们计划的轨道上来。

00:22:57.970 --> 00:23:14.950
<v Elna Baker>She was not going to allow this obstacle to push them apart. She jumped into action. This was before you could Google everything, so Michelle went to the library and did research on adult literacy. She broke it down into manageable steps and wrote out a timeline for him.
<v Elna Baker>她不打算让这个障碍把他们分开。她立即采取行动。这发生在Google还没普及的时候，所以米歇尔去了图书馆，研究成人扫盲。她把它分解成可管理的步骤，并为他写了一个时间表。

00:23:14.950 --> 00:23:39.050
<v Michelle Buteau>My game plan for him was I had a list of places he could go to to go to night school, a therapist he could talk to, easy adult reading books, tips and tricks. It was like a whole sort of care package of how to just take it on. And he was really overwhelmed by it. I don't know. It's like, OK, this is a really big deal. I get it.
<v Michelle Buteau>我的计划是，列出一个他可以去参加夜校的名单，一位他可以交谈的治疗师，简单的成人阅读书目，小窍门。就像一个完整的关怀包，告诉他该怎么解决这个问题。他被这些弄得不知所措。我不知道。就是，好吧，这是一件大事。我明白。

00:23:39.050 --> 00:24:10.390
<v Elna Baker>Michelle's dyslexic, and her boyfriend didn't have a learning disability. But still, she could empathize with how hard it can be to read. And at first, Michelle's boyfriend was totally on board with the plan. But after a year of Michelle offering him solutions, he still hadn't taken any action. It just seemed like he didn't want to. He got around the world just fine without reading. She started realizing, oh wait, his illiteracy was a way bigger problem for her than it was for him, and it really started pushing them apart.
<v Elna Baker>米歇尔有阅读障碍，而她男友没有学习障碍。但即便如此，她也能体会到阅读有多难。起初，米歇尔的男友完全赞成这个计划。但在米歇尔提供了一年解决方案后，他仍然没有任何行动。看起来他根本不想学。他不用识字也能在世界上过得挺好。她开始意识到，等等，他的文盲问题对她来说比对他自己要严重得多，这真的开始让他们分道扬镳。

00:24:10.390 --> 00:24:31.960
<v Michelle Buteau>I stopped being his girlfriend and sort of became his coach or his mom, and it wasn't fun for either one of us. I tried every tactic. I was patient. I was nice. I was stern. And then I kind of backed off. I'm like, whatever. He needs to do on his own time.
<v Michelle Buteau>我不再是他的女朋友，反而成了他的教练或者妈，这对我们俩来说都不好玩。我尝试了每一种策略。我耐心。我友善。我严厉。然后我退出了。我想，随便吧。他得按照自己的节奏去做。

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:51.720
<v Elna Baker>It started to bleed its way into every moment they shared together, like she could never fully relax anymore. Even when they were happy, she'd snap herself out of it and think, wait, no, no. We're forgetting that there's this huge, looming problem, and we've got to fix it before everything can be OK. She started to resent him.
<v Elna Baker>这开始渗透到他们共同生活的每一个时刻，她再也无法完全放松了。即使他们开心的时候，她也会突然警醒并想，等等，不，不。我们忘了有一个巨大的、迫在眉睫的问题，我们得在一切好起来之前解决它。她开始怨恨他。

00:24:51.720 --> 00:25:07.240
<v Michelle Buteau>We don't even laugh anymore. We're not even holding hands like we used to. We're not even having sex like we used to, simply because you're not even going to this class. Like, if you just went to a class, I would just be so happy.
