<v Marissa Kohan>于是他拆开信，读了整封信。按照我爸的说法，我妈的前未婚夫正在表达对她永恒的爱，恳求她不要走，说他想和她破镜重圆。我爸当时就炸了。

00:31:30.430 --> 00:31:41.610
<v Ira Glass>He decided, this is it. It's now or never. He decides he's going to meet her in Dallas, which is where she is in her car trip, and also is where the ex-fiance lives. He hurries to the airport.
<v Ira Glass>他决定，就是现在，机不可失。他决定去达拉斯接她，那是她开车路过的地方，也是前未婚夫住的地方。他匆忙赶往机场。

00:31:41.610 --> 00:32:07.630
<v Marissa Kohan>The way he told it was he was going through the airport, you know, running through the Florida airport. Gets a ring out of a little gumball machine. Puts, you know, however much that costs, a quarter or whatever, in the gumball machine, pulls out a Mickey Mouse ring-- because it's Florida, naturally. Gets on the plane. Flies and meets my mom in Dallas. And right there in the airport, gets down on one knee the second he sees her.
<v Marissa Kohan>按照他的描述，他冲进机场，你知道，在佛罗里达机场奔跑。从一个小口香糖机里弄出一枚戒指。投入了，不管那要多少钱，一角硬币或者什么的，放进机子，掏出一枚米老鼠戒指——因为是在佛罗里达，很自然。上了飞机。飞到达拉斯见到我妈。就在机场里，一见到她就单膝下跪。

00:32:07.630 --> 00:32:11.950
<v Debbie>I had no plans of seeing the ex-fiance at all, whatsoever.
<v Debbie>我完全没打算见那个前未婚夫，一点都没。

00:32:11.950 --> 00:32:22.920
<v Ira Glass>This is Marissa's mom, Debbie. She had no idea the ex-fiance had written a letter or that Ron had opened it. All she knew was that he had offered to drive the rest of the way across the country with her and was going to meet her in the Dallas airport.
<v Ira Glass>这是玛丽莎的妈妈，黛比。她根本不知道前未婚夫写了信，也不知道罗恩拆了它。她只知道他提议开车陪她走完剩下的路，并要在达拉斯机场见她。

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:45.920
<v Debbie>He gets off the plane, and I greet him. And practically the very next thing out of his mouth is, will you marry me? And he opens up his hand. And in his hand, he's got this little Mickey Mouse ring in his hand. And I am not proud of my reaction at all. [LAUGHS] Do you want to hear it?
<v Debbie>他下了飞机，我去迎接他。他嘴里说的几乎下一句话就是，你愿意嫁给我吗？他摊开手。他手里握着那枚小小的米老鼠戒指。我一点也不为自己的反应感到骄傲。（笑）你想听听吗？

00:32:45.920 --> 00:32:47.010
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>想。

00:32:47.010 --> 00:32:54.400
<v Debbie>[LAUGHS] My reaction was, are you crazy? This is the craziest thing I've ever heard of. I barely know you. That was my reaction.
<v Debbie>（笑）我的反应是，你疯了吗？这是我听过最疯狂的事。我几乎不了解你。这就是我的反应。

00:32:54.400 --> 00:32:56.820
<v Ira Glass>I have to say, that is my favorite part of the story.
<v Ira Glass>我得说，这是我最喜欢的故事部分。

00:32:56.820 --> 00:32:58.780
<v Debbie>I could have been a little more gentle.
<v Debbie>我本来可以温和一点的。

00:32:58.780 --> 00:33:06.050
<v Ira Glass>No, what I love about it is that in the movies, when somebody does a gesture like that, often it's way crazier than what he did.
<v Ira Glass>不，我喜欢它的是，在电影里，当某人做出那样的举动时，通常比他做的还要疯狂。

00:33:06.050 --> 00:33:07.380
<v Debbie>Yes.
<v Debbie>是的。

00:33:07.380 --> 00:33:21.440
<v Ira Glass>And kind of stalker-ish. But the movie just acts like, oh, that was a totally lovely thing for a person to do because true love will out. And I like that you had the normal human reaction that a normal person would have. It would be like, are you nuts?
<v Ira Glass>而且有点跟踪狂的感觉。但电影只是把它表现为，噢，那是一个人能做的非常可爱的事，因为真爱总会显现。我喜欢你有正常人会有的反应。那反应就像是，你神经病吗？

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:28.690
<v Debbie>Right. Right. Right. Right. Yeah. Well, I'm too pragmatic to fall for that one, I guess.
<v Debbie>对。对。对。对。是的。嗯，我太现实了，没法被那一招骗倒，我想。

00:33:28.690 --> 00:33:42.050
<v Ira Glass>True love eventually did win out though. At their wedding, in addition to a regular ring, she also wore the Mickey Mouse one.
<v Ira Glass>真爱最终还是获胜了。在他们的婚礼上，除了普通的戒指，她还戴上了那枚米老鼠戒指。

00:33:42.050 --> 00:34:00.820
<v Ira Glass>There was another story we heard about somebody running to win love, which actually kind of paralleled the run that happens at the end of Neil's favorite rom-com, When Harry Met Sally. In When Harry Met Sally, the way it goes is that it's New Year's Eve. Harry's wandering around the streets of New York, and he realizes that he loves Sally, has to tell her now, breaks into a run to go tell her.
<v Ira Glass>我们听到了另一个关于某人奔跑去赢取爱情的故事，这其实有点像尼尔最喜欢的浪漫喜剧《当哈利遇到莎莉》结尾的奔跑。《当哈利遇到莎莉》里，故事发生在新年前夜。哈利在纽约街头徘徊，他意识到自己爱莎莉，必须现在告诉她，于是冲刺去告诉她。

00:34:00.820 --> 00:34:06.550
<v Neil Drumming>It's very New York-centric in this one because he tries to catch a cab, and of course he can't-- so he's just going to run the entire distance.
<v Neil Drumming>这一幕非常以纽约为中心，因为他试图拦出租车，当然他拦不到——所以他决定全程跑过去。

00:34:06.550 --> 00:34:12.080
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum has our real-life version of this story, about a guy named Steve Snyder.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆带给我们这个故事的现实版本，关于一个叫史蒂夫·斯奈德的人。

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:35.370
<v David  Kestenbaum>The thought that someone actually made one of these runs in real life seems so unlikely to me that I wanted to see where it had happened, retrace the steps of it. So I met Steve where the run began, at this burger place on Ludlow Street in New York City. Steve is the kind of guy who is not very good at hiding his feelings. Like, if this were a movie, you could title it "Say Everything."
<v David  Kestenbaum>一个人在现实中真的做出这种奔跑的想法对我来说太不可思议了，所以我想要去看看它发生的地方，追溯它的脚步。所以我在史蒂夫奔跑的起点——纽约卢多街的一家汉堡店见到了他。史蒂夫属于那种不善于隐藏感情的人。如果这是一部电影，你可以把它命名为“有话直说”。

00:34:35.370 --> 00:34:40.170
<v David  Kestenbaum>The setup to the run is this, he'd met this woman, Emily, at a birthday party. He was totally smitten.
<v David  Kestenbaum>奔跑的前奏是：他在一个生日派对上遇到了这个女人，艾米丽。他彻底沦陷了。

00:34:40.170 --> 00:34:47.770
<v Steve Snyder>What I remember is the party kind of turned into sort of a tunnel vision moment. All I really did was talk to her.
<v Steve Snyder>我记得那场聚会变成了一种隧道视野的时刻。我唯一做的就是跟她聊天。

00:34:47.770 --> 00:34:53.050
<v David Kestenbaum>It didn't go great. He kept asking her, you want to go to a movie? Maybe we could hang out sometime.
<v David Kestenbaum>进行得并不顺利。他一直问她，想去看电影吗？也许我们什么时候可以一起玩？

00:34:53.050 --> 00:34:56.570
<v Steve Snyder>If there's a trajectory of my life, it's going from clingy to a little less clingy.
<v Steve Snyder>如果我的人生有一条轨迹，那就是从死缠烂打到稍微不那么死缠烂打。

00:34:56.570 --> 00:35:06.480
<v David Kestenbaum>Steve emailed her after the party. Nothing came of it.
<v David Kestenbaum>史蒂夫在派对后发邮件给她。没什么结果。

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:30.630
<v David Kestenbaum>A year passes. And just like in When Harry Met Sally, they meet a second time. In Steve and Emily's case, it's at the very same birthday party, same apartment. And they become friends. It was the kind of friendship where, really, it could go either way. She seemed kind of interested in something more, but maybe not enough. And the longer they were friends, it was like, well, maybe that's what they were.
<v David Kestenbaum>一年过去了。就像《当哈利遇到莎莉》里一样，他们第二次见面了。在史蒂夫和艾米丽的例子中，是在同一个生日派对，同一个公寓。他们成了朋友。那是一种真的可能走向任何方向的友谊。她看起来对进一步发展有点兴趣，但也许不够。他们当朋友越久，就越像是，嗯，也许他们就是这样了。

00:35:30.630 --> 00:35:37.320
<v David Kestenbaum>Steve had a job as a film critic, so they would go to movies, lots of movies, where they would not hold hands and not kiss.
<v David Kestenbaum>史蒂夫的工作是电影评论员，所以他们会去看电影，看很多电影，在那里他们不牵手也不接吻。

00:35:37.320 --> 00:35:53.650
<v Steve Snyder>There is a pathetic moment. So Lincoln Center Station, we're waiting for the train. It's like some crazy, 20-minute wait where the words actually come out of my mouth, you know, hey, if you ever want a film critic as a boyfriend, you just let me know.
<v Steve Snyder>有一个悲惨的时刻。在林肯中心站，我们等火车。那是那种疯狂的20分钟等待，我嘴里竟然蹦出那句话，你知道，嘿，如果你什么时候想要个电影评论员当男友，你就告诉我。

00:35:53.650 --> 00:35:54.810
<v Steve Snyder>[LAUGHTER]
<v Steve Snyder>（笑声）

00:35:54.810 --> 00:36:00.390
<v Steve Snyder>I think we were talking about-- it fit into the context of the conversation somehow, but I can't think of anything more pathetic.
<v Steve Snyder>我想当时是在谈论——它以某种方式契合了对话背景，但我实在想不出比这更悲惨的事了。

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:03.600
<v David Kestenbaum>What was her reaction?
<v David Kestenbaum>她的反应是什么？

00:36:03.600 --> 00:36:06.590
<v Steve Snyder>She just kind of laughed and was like, yeah, OK, OK.
<v Steve Snyder>她只是笑了笑，说，嗯，好吧，好吧。

00:36:06.590 --> 00:36:20.680
<v David Kestenbaum>The run happened on a night where they were not going to hang out. Steve thinks he was working on a review of some Mutant Ninja Turtle movie. And after work, he goes out with some friends to a music place.
<v David Kestenbaum>那次奔跑发生在一个他们原本不会见面的一天晚上。史蒂夫想他当时正在写一篇关于某部忍者神龟电影的影评。工作结束后，他和朋友们去了音乐场所。

00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:34.670
<v Steve Snyder>Music's so loud and it's kind of fun. You know, it's a great night. And we're dancing. I actually started dancing for once. We leave the place. We're going to go get some greasy food because we're hungry and we haven't eaten. And I have my bag and everything, so we walk into the burger joint.
<v Steve Snyder>音乐很大声，挺有趣的。你知道，那是一个很棒的夜晚。我们在跳舞。我破天荒地开始跳舞。我们离开了那里。我们要去吃点油腻的东西，因为我们饿了，什么都没吃。我带着包什么的，所以我们走进了这家汉堡店。

00:36:34.670 --> 00:36:42.860
<v David Kestenbaum>The place we are sitting in right now-- greasy spoon, tiled white walls and painted brick. It's like a piece of a subway station that's somehow above ground.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们现在坐的这个地方——路边摊，铺着瓷砖的白墙和粉刷过的砖块。感觉就像一个地下的地铁站不知怎么跑到了地面上。

00:36:42.860 --> 00:37:01.160
<v Steve Snyder>You know, there's what, five tables here? It's tiny. And so I remember just kind of throwing my bag down. And then I pull out my phone just to check. And it's one of those weird nights, right? Like, I just haven't checked my phone almost any night-- any night, 99% of nights, you know, your phone's right next to you. If it's buzzing, you hear it. If it rings, you hear it.
<v Steve Snyder>你知道，这里有几张桌子？这里很小。我记得就把包一扔。然后我掏出手机看了看。那天晚上挺怪的，对吧？我基本上整晚都没看手机——任何晚上，99%的时间，你的手机就在身边。如果它震动，你会听到。如果它响了，你会听到。

00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:19.500
<v Steve Snyder>I don't know if I had bad reception in the place because it's kind of an older building, but I looked down, and I swear to god, I have like 22 text messages. And I'm like, what? And they're all from Emily. And so I run out of the burger place to the street.
<v Steve Snyder>我不知道是因为它是老建筑导致收讯不好，但我低头一看，天哪，我有22条短信。我心想，什么？它们全来自艾米丽。于是我从汉堡店冲到街上。

00:37:19.500 --> 00:37:23.000
<v David Kestenbaum>Right, let's go outside.
<v David Kestenbaum>好吧，去外面说。

00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:25.670
<v Steve Snyder>[LAUGHS] OK.
<v Steve Snyder>（笑）好。

00:37:25.670 --> 00:37:31.320
<v David   Kestenbaum>All right, so you rush out here.
<v David   Kestenbaum>好，所以你冲了出来。

00:37:31.320 --> 00:37:42.450
<v Steve Snyder>I rush out here, and there's not exactly a lot of room. I mean, there's not room at all. So this is a crowded-- like, this is where you hang out till 4:00 AM.
<v Steve Snyder>我冲了出来，这里空间并不大。我是说，根本没地方。所以这是一个拥挤的地方——这是你一直聚会到凌晨4点的地方。

00:37:42.450 --> 00:38:05.770
<v Steve Snyder>So I rush out and I start reading through the messages. And it starts very kind of innocently, very like, hey, what are you up to tonight? Oh, are you-- oh, maybe you're out? I was wondering if maybe you wanted to like get a drink or something? And then it starts escalating like, wait, are you not texting me? Or why aren't you texting me back?
<v Steve Snyder>所以我就冲了出来，开始阅读这些信息。一开始很天真，比如，嘿，你今晚在干嘛？噢，你在……噢，也许你出去了？我想知道你是否想去喝一杯什么的？然后开始升级，比如，等等，你为什么不回我信息？或者你为什么不理我？

00:38:05.770 --> 00:38:07.810
<v David Kestenbaum>Usually, he was pretty quick at getting back to her.
<v David Kestenbaum>通常他回复她挺快的。

00:38:07.810 --> 00:38:18.600
<v Steve Snyder>Because I was totally into her. And then it started being like-- it started getting a little more paranoid. And then I think she started thinking I was on a date or something.
<v Steve Snyder>因为我完全被她迷住了。然后它开始变得……变得有点偏执。然后我想她开始觉得我在约会什么的。

00:38:18.600 --> 00:38:27.500
<v David Kestenbaum>In just two hours, it looked like she'd gone through all these phases, everything laid out in all these texts, ending with one that Steve was not expecting.
<v David Kestenbaum>仅仅两个小时内，看起来她经历了一切阶段，所有这些都在短信里体现出来，最后一条史蒂夫完全没料到。

00:38:27.500 --> 00:38:48.630
<v Steve Snyder>And the last text message said, maybe we need to talk about this whole not dating thing. I just-- I-- I thought, this is it. Like whatever's about to happen, this is the moment. And so I immediately called her. I immediately just hit dial.
<v Steve Snyder>最后一条短信说，也许我们需要谈谈这整个“不约会”的事情。我只是……我想，就是现在了。不管发生什么，就是这一刻了。于是我立即拨了回去。我立即按了拨号。

00:38:48.630 --> 00:39:03.290
<v Steve Snyder>I didn't quite know what I was going to say. And I think she picked up the phone, said, hello, and I just kind of started going into it, like, I don't know what to say here. Like, yes, I want to date you. And what have we been doing?
<v Steve Snyder>我不太知道我要说什么。我想她接起电话说，喂，我只是开始说了，比如，我不知道该说什么。比如，是的，我想和你约会。我们一直在干什么？

00:39:03.290 --> 00:39:31.040
<v Steve Snyder>You know, I'm screaming in the street. The cars aren't moving. People stop walking by me. They just stop to see what's going on here, because I'm screaming like, I love you. I don't know how to be clearer, like, I love you. And so I'm screaming this. And this crowd is starting to cheer me on. Someone does yell, like, say you love him! Say you love him, yelling to her through the phone. And--
<v Steve Snyder>你知道，我在街上尖叫。车都没动。路人停下脚步，他们停下来看发生了什么，因为我在尖叫比如“我爱你”。我不知道怎么说得更清楚，我爱你。所以我尖叫着这个。人群开始为我欢呼。有人大喊，比如，说你也爱他！说你也爱他，对着电话朝她喊。然后——

00:39:31.040 --> 00:39:34.850
<v David   Kestenbaum>I love New York. [LAUGHS]
<v David   Kestenbaum>我爱纽约。（笑）

00:39:34.850 --> 00:39:57.630
<v Steve  Snyder>And all I remember her saying, and it might have been all that she did say, was, you need to stop yelling. If you want to talk about this, you might as well just come here. And as far as I'm concerned, this is the moment-- like, it's on. This is happening. And I just start running down the street looking for cabs.
<v Steve  Snyder>我记得她说的唯一一句话，也许也是她说的全部，就是，你需要停止尖叫。如果你想谈这个，你最好直接过来。在我看来，就是这一刻——开始了。这就是现实。于是我开始奔跑，寻找出租车。

00:39:57.630 --> 00:39:59.480
<v David Kestenbaum>Let's run. Let's run.
<v David Kestenbaum>跑吧，跑起来。

00:39:59.480 --> 00:40:01.780
<v Steve Snyder>[LAUGHS] OK. So I start running.
<v Steve Snyder>（笑）好。所以我就开始跑。

00:40:01.780 --> 00:40:05.350
<v David Kestenbaum>We are now jogging up Ludlow Street. It's actually kind of exciting.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们现在正在卢多街上慢跑。其实挺刺激的。

00:40:05.350 --> 00:40:23.700
<v Steve Snyder>The crowd's looking at me, wondering what the hell I'm doing. And literally, I'm banging on every cab, because it's that time of night where some are just saying they're off duty. So I think I hit a couple. And I'm sort of like, can you take me just up to the East Village, not that far?
<v Steve Snyder>人群看着我，纳闷我到底在干什么。我真的在敲每一辆出租车，因为那个点有的司机说他们下班了。我想我敲了好几辆。我当时就在想，你能带我去东村吗，又不远？

00:40:23.700 --> 00:40:27.970
<v David Kestenbaum>But taxi after taxi is like, no, or there's someone in it. So he keeps running.
<v David Kestenbaum>但一辆接一辆的出租车都说不行，或者里面已经有人了。所以他继续跑。

00:40:27.970 --> 00:40:40.730
<v Steve Snyder>It's like I didn't tell my friends where I was going. I left my laptop, all these screeners that I had taken assignments to review, that the movie studios told me they needed back. I ditched everything.
<v Steve Snyder>就像我没告诉朋友我要去哪儿。我丢下了笔记本电脑，丢下了电影公司交代的要回去复审的所有审片。我丢下了一切。

00:40:40.730 --> 00:40:42.170
<v David Kestenbaum>Can we talk about the running?
<v David Kestenbaum>我们能聊聊那个奔跑吗？

00:40:42.170 --> 00:40:43.260
<v Steve Snyder>Yeah.
<v Steve Snyder>可以。

00:40:43.260 --> 00:40:45.260
<v David Kestenbaum>Why does love always involve running?
<v David Kestenbaum>为什么爱情总是涉及奔跑？

00:40:45.260 --> 00:40:49.720
<v Steve Snyder>[LAUGHS] That is interesting. Why did I feel like I had to run?
<v Steve Snyder>（笑）那真有意思。为什么我觉得我非得跑不可？

00:40:49.720 --> 00:40:54.460
<v David Kestenbaum>In the movies, they run, but usually it's because someone's about to get married or about to get on a plane.
<v David Kestenbaum>在电影里，他们奔跑，但通常是因为有人要结婚或者要登机。

00:40:54.460 --> 00:41:05.300
<v Steve Snyder>It felt very urgent to get there very quick. This had been building up for years. And for a moment, she was willing to consider it. And I was going to get there before she said it was too late or she was too tired.
<v Steve Snyder>觉得必须快点赶到那里真的很迫切。这已经是几年来的积压了。有一刻，她愿意考虑一下。而我要赶在她改口说“太晚了”或者她太累了之前赶到。

00:41:05.300 --> 00:41:26.830
<v David Kestenbaum>Steve told me he'd been living in the friend universe for so long, and now it was like this little wormhole had opened up-- he didn't know for how long-- where he might be able to slip into the parallel universe of boyfriend. Steve did eventually get a taxi, he made it to her apartment, and he stayed over. And they did become boyfriend and girlfriend.
<v David Kestenbaum>史蒂夫告诉我，他在“朋友宇宙”里待了太久，现在就像一个小虫洞打开了——他不知道会持续多久——他也许能滑进“男友的平行宇宙”。史蒂夫最终拦到了一辆出租车，赶到了她的公寓，并在那里过夜了。他们确实成了情侣。

00:41:26.830 --> 00:41:42.430
