00:53:05.580 --> 00:53:14.430
<v Diane Wu>She didn't. Geoffrey offered to help Jillian hail a cab, but she was like, no, it's fine. I'll just take the subway. She walked to the station, totally miserable.
<v Diane Wu>她没留下。杰弗里提出帮吉莉安拦出租车，但她说，不用，没关系。我会坐地铁。她走向车站，心情糟糕透顶。

00:53:14.430 --> 00:53:27.000
<v Jillian Welsh>Then it really hits me that I have stolen this man's sheets. How do you come back from that? How do you-- how are you not the weird girl who took his bedsheets?
<v Jillian Welsh>然后我真的意识到我偷了这个男人的床单。这该怎么补救？你怎么能不成为那个偷了他床单的怪女孩？

00:53:27.000 --> 00:53:27.810
<v Diane Wu>Yeah.
<v Diane Wu>是啊。

00:53:27.810 --> 00:53:49.330
<v Jillian Welsh>So then I'm so inside myself and I hear this voice being like, ma'am, excuse me, ma'am. And I look up. And in New York, they have this station outside of subway entrances with this folding table and the NYPD stands behind. And it's a random bag search.
<v Jillian Welsh>所以我正沉浸在自我怀疑里，听到一个声音说，女士，不好意思，女士。我抬头看。在纽约，他们会在地铁入口外设有个折叠桌，纽约警察站在后面。这是随机检查包。

00:53:49.330 --> 00:53:50.690
<v Diane Wu>No!
<v Diane Wu>不！

00:53:50.690 --> 00:53:57.740
<v Diane  Wu>And here, Jillian does what any sensible leading lady would do when confronted by law enforcement.
<v Diane  Wu>在这里，吉莉安做了任何一位精明的女主角在面对执法人员时都会做的事。

00:53:57.740 --> 00:54:08.530
<v Jillian Welsh>I pretend I don't hear them. And I try to exit the subway station in like a brisk, like, run-walk. And nothing looks more suspicious than trying to avoid a random bag search.
<v Jillian Welsh>我假装没听见他们。我试图以一种轻快、竞走的方式离开地铁站。没有任何东西比试图逃避随机包检查看起来更可疑了。

00:54:08.530 --> 00:54:09.940
<v Diane Wu>I'm sure. I'm sure.
<v Diane Wu>我肯定。我肯定。

00:54:09.940 --> 00:54:11.040
<v Jillian Welsh>Yeah. Oh.
<v Jillian Welsh>是啊。噢。

00:54:11.040 --> 00:54:18.290
<v Diane  Wu>The officer catches up to Jillian, unzips her backpack, and pulls out the sheets, which are covered in blood.
<v Diane  Wu>警察追上吉莉安，拉开她的背包拉链，掏出了沾满鲜血的床单。

00:54:18.290 --> 00:54:36.570
<v Jillian Welsh>I remember him-- and the subway has such distinct lighting, like I just remember him holding up these sheets, my menstrual sheets of shame, like menstrual sheets of doom. I realize that they didn't look like menstrual sheets of doom, they looked like murder sheets of doom.
<v Jillian Welsh>我记得他——地铁里的灯光很独特，我记得他举着这些床单，我羞耻的经血床单，就像末日的经血床单。我意识到它们看起来不像末日的经血床单，它们看起来像末日的谋杀床单。

00:54:36.570 --> 00:54:41.060
<v Jillian Welsh>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jillian Welsh>（笑声）

00:54:41.060 --> 00:55:02.340
<v Jillian Welsh>He asked me to explain it, and I just start crying. And I can barely get the words out. I'm just trying to explain to him, it's my period on those sheets. And I stole the sheets from the guy that I was with. And I know that that's wrong. Like I know that the actual theft of the sheets is wrong. And I promised the officer that I would return them, but I just needed to wash them first.
<v Jillian Welsh>他让我解释，我当时就开始哭。话都说不出来。我只是试图向他解释，那是床单上的生理期经血。而我偷了那床单，从跟我在一起的那个男人那儿。我知道那是错的。我知道实际的偷窃行为是错的。我向警察保证我会归还它们，但我只是需要先洗洗。

00:55:02.340 --> 00:55:18.090
<v Diane Wu>The police officer looks Jillian up and down, asks for her ID, and points her to go stand in a little box taped on the floor by the wall. She watches him walk over to his partner and have a very serious-looking conversation. The sheets stay crumpled up on the plastic folding table.
<v Diane Wu>警察打量了吉莉安一番，要了她的证件，并让她去站在折叠桌旁墙上贴好的小盒子里。她看着他走过去和他同伴进行了一场看起来很严肃的谈话。床单团成一团放在塑料折叠桌上。

00:55:18.090 --> 00:55:40.050
<v Jillian Welsh>Then he comes back, and gives me an ultimatum. So we can go down to the precinct and they can file a report and they've got to keep the sheets just in case it's evidence, you know, and they're going to ask me a bunch of questions and follow up, or I can take him back to the apartment, the apartment that I just came from, and have my partner corroborate my story.
<v Jillian Welsh>然后他回来，给了我最后通牒。我们可以去警察局，他们可以备案，他们得扣押床单以防作为证据，你知道，他们会问我一堆问题并追踪调查，或者我可以带他回刚才那个公寓，带我去的那间，让我男友为我的话做旁证。

00:55:40.050 --> 00:55:41.800
<v Diane  Wu>Oh, my god.
<v Diane  Wu>天哪。

00:55:41.800 --> 00:56:06.280
<v Jillian Welsh>And I had to think about it. Like, I honestly-- like, I honestly gave it a really solid, good think. There was a huge part of me that would rather go to the police station than have to go back and show Jeffery these-- not only show him these sheets, but also bring the police there. But, you know, my common sense caught up with me of, this looks like I've done something very wrong.
<v Jillian Welsh>我得考虑一下。我真心……我真心给了它一个非常扎实、认真的考虑。我内心有很大一部分宁可去警察局，也不想回去告诉杰弗里这些——不仅仅是向他展示这些床单，还得带警察过去。但是，你知道，我的常识占了上风，这看起来像我做了什么非常严重的事。

00:56:06.280 --> 00:56:19.690
<v Diane Wu>They make the long walk to Geoffrey's apartment, silently. They get to the doorstep and buzz, wait for Geoffrey to walk down five flights of stairs. When he gets there, the police officer does all the talking.
<v Diane Wu>他们沉默地走了很长的路回到杰弗里的公寓。走到门口按响门铃，等着杰弗里走下五层楼。当他到达时，警察说了所有的话。

00:56:19.690 --> 00:56:40.480
<v Diane Wu>Good evening, do you know this woman? Geoffrey says he does, that they'd just been hanging out. Then the cop reaches into Jillian's backpack and pulls out the sheets. Do you know what these are? Geoffrey says, those are my sheets. The police officer asks, can you identify the substance on these sheets?
<v Diane Wu>晚上好，你认识这个女人吗？杰弗里说认识，他们刚才一起玩的。然后警察伸手进吉莉安的背包掏出床单。你知道这是什么吗？杰弗里说，那是我的床单。警察问，你能辨认出床单上的物质吗？

00:56:40.480 --> 00:57:09.760
<v Jillian  Welsh>And I just remember being fascinated, because without hesitating and almost in this stronger voice, he just says, menstrual fluid, which is like-- you know, I would call my period anything other than what it actually was. So for him to look a police officer right in the eyes and just say very confidently, menstrual fluid, it was just-- it sounded so scientific and very grown up.
<v Jillian  Welsh>我记得我很着迷，因为他没有犹豫，而且声音几乎变得更有力，他只是说：经血。那感觉就像——你知道，我过去会把我的生理期称为除了真实情况之外的任何东西。所以他看着警察的眼睛，非常自信地说是“经血”，那听起来如此科学，非常成熟。

00:57:09.760 --> 00:57:20.300
<v Diane  Wu>The cops, having fulfilled their narrative purpose and successfully reunited our couple, leave.
<v Diane  Wu>警察履行了他们的叙事目的并成功让我们的情侣团聚后，离开了。

00:57:20.300 --> 00:57:33.420
<v Jillian Welsh>And then I'm alone with Geoffrey. And I'm trying to apologize to him, namely for stealing his sheets, but also just for everything, for bringing the police back to his house.
<v Jillian Welsh>然后我就和杰弗里独处了。我试图向他道歉，主要是因为偷了他的床单，也为了这一切，因为把警察带回他家。

00:57:33.420 --> 00:57:44.200
<v Diane Wu>Jillian remembers sobbing through all of this. She's mortified. All of a sudden, Geoffrey stops her and says this thing that still hits Jillian in a soft spot.
<v Diane Wu>吉莉安记得哭着说完了这一切。她丢脸死了。突然，杰弗里打断她，说了这句话，这句话至今仍击中吉莉安柔软的地方。

00:57:44.200 --> 00:58:08.140
<v Jillian Welsh>He told me-- I just remember him looking at me and he's like, you're so strange. [LAUGHS] He said, wonderfully strange. Everything that you could possibly think of going wrong went wrong, and it all still turned out OK. He still liked me, no matter what.
<v Jillian Welsh>他告诉我——我记得看着他，他说：你真奇怪。（笑）他说，美妙地奇怪。所有可能出错的事情都出错了，但最后一切都还好。无论如何，他依然喜欢我。

00:58:08.140 --> 00:58:37.270
<v Diane  Wu>This is like my favorite moment in any rom-com. It's the scene in 10 Things I Hate About You when Julia Stiles reads her poem to Heath Ledger in front of the entire class. It's Jack Nicholson in As Good As It Gets telling Helen Hunt how he just can't believe she runs into strangers all day long, and they don't know that they just met the greatest woman alive. And of course, it's Mark Darcy at the bottom of the stairs telling Bridget Jones, I like you very much, just as you are.
<v Diane  Wu>这是我最喜欢的浪漫喜剧瞬间。那是《我恨你的十件事》中朱莉娅·斯泰尔斯在全班面前给希斯·莱杰读诗的一幕。那是《尽善尽美》中杰克·尼科尔森告诉海伦·亨特他是多么无法相信她整天遇到陌生人，而他们不知道自己遇到了世上最伟大的女性。当然，还有《BJ单身日记》结尾马克·达西站在楼梯下对她说：我很喜欢你，就喜欢现在的你。

00:58:37.270 --> 00:58:50.370
<v Diane  Wu>To me, the whole point of rom-coms is to set up that line. It's what we all want to hear and say to the people we love most. But real life doesn't guarantee a plot line that pushes us to say it.
<v Diane  Wu>对我来说，浪漫喜剧的全部意义就是铺垫那句台词。那是我们都想听到并对我们最爱的人说的话。但现实生活并不能保证一个推动我们去说出它的情节。

00:58:50.370 --> 00:59:23.410
<v Ira    Glass>Diane Wu, she's one of the producers of our show. Jillian and Geoffrey dated for a few months, and then they broke it off when real life, new jobs, her expired visa from Canada, got in the way. She says she's glad it ended before anything bad happened. Keeps the memory sweet, just like a movie. Today, Jillian Welsh is a comedian in Toronto. A version of this story first aired on the RISK! Podcast.
<v Ira    Glass>戴安·吴，她是我们的制作人之一。吉莉安和杰弗里约会了几个月，然后在现实生活——新工作、她来自加拿大的过期签证——横插一脚时分手了。她说她很高兴一切在坏事发生前结束了。保留了甜蜜的回忆，就像一部电影。如今，吉莉安·威尔什是多伦多的一名喜剧演员。这个故事的版本最初在 RISK! 播客上播出。

00:59:23.410 --> 01:00:13.020
<v Ira    Glass>Our program is produced today by Neil Drumming and Diane Wu. The people who put our show together includes Elna Baker, Elise Bergerson, Ben Calhoun, Dana Chivvis, Sean Cole, Whitney Dangerfield, Stephanie Foo, Damien Grave, Kimberly Henderson, Chana Joffe-Walt, David Kestenbaum, Seth Lind, Alvin Melathe, BA Parker, Ben Phelan, Robyn Semien, Alissa Shipp, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierny, and Julie Whitaker. Our senior producer is Brian Reed. Our managing editor is Susan Burton.
<v Ira    Glass>我们的节目由尼尔·德拉明和戴安·吴制作。节目组成员包括埃尔娜·贝克、埃莉斯·伯格森、本·卡尔霍恩、达纳·奇维斯、肖恩·科尔、惠特尼·丹格菲尔德、斯蒂芬妮·福、达米恩·格雷夫、金伯利·亨德森、查娜·乔夫-沃尔特、大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆、赛斯·林德、阿尔文·梅拉特、BA·帕克、本·菲兰、罗宾·塞米恩、阿丽莎·希普、克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉、马特·蒂尔尼和朱莉·惠特克。高级制作人是布莱恩·里德。主编是苏珊·伯顿。

01:00:13.020 --> 01:00:14.020
<v Ira    Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira    Glass>（鸣谢）

01:00:14.020 --> 01:00:21.280
<v Ira    Glass>Our website, thisamericanlife.org, This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange.
<v Ira    Glass>我们的网站 thisamericanlife.org，《美国生活》由 PRX 公共广播交换平台发送至各公共广播电台。

01:00:21.280 --> 01:00:42.430
<v Ira    Glass>Quick program note, our colleagues at our home station, WBEZ Chicago have a new podcast called Making Obama about Barack Obama's early years in Chicago, which features him, his friends, mentors, and rivals talking about his years in Chicago and his rise. It's six episodes, stuff I have never heard or read before about him. Making Obama, get it wherever you get your podcasts.
<v Ira    Glass>节目快讯，我们在母台芝加哥WBEZ的同事有一个名为《Making Obama》的新播客，讲述巴拉克·奥巴马在芝加哥的早年生活，由他本人、朋友、导师和竞争对手讲述他在芝加哥岁月及崛起的历程。共有六集，里面有很多我从未听说或读过的关于他的事。《Making Obama》，在你获取播客的地方即可收听。

01:00:42.430 --> 01:00:49.940
<v Ira    Glass>Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder Mr. Torey Malatia. You know, he and I were reminiscing about that taxi ride we had years ago in England with Margaret Thatcher.
<v Ira    Glass>一如既往地感谢本节目的联合创始人托雷·马拉蒂亚先生。你知道，当年在英国，我和他曾回忆起那次和玛格丽特·撒切尔一起坐的出租车。

01:00:49.940 --> 01:00:54.020
<v Jillian Welsh>And I think that's probably the first time I'd ever made out with anybody in the back of a cab.
<v Jillian Welsh>而且那可能是我第一次在出租车后座跟人激吻。

01:00:54.020 --> 01:00:59.020
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Happy Valentine's Day. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。情人节快乐。下周同一时间带来更多《美国生活》的故事。
